            Egg white is the fourth route and a good one: complete protein, no soy, no dairy and a neutral taste. Our <a href="/best-egg-white-protein-powder/">egg white protein powder</a> guide ranks the options, and Naked Egg in particular is a single-ingredient powder.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="why-avoid-soy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Why Avoid Soy? Allergy, Hormones and Thyroid
          </h2>

          <p>
            People search for soy-free protein for several reasons, and they are not equally well supported. Allergy is the clearest: soy is one of the nine major food allergens in the US, and a diagnosed soy allergy is a good reason to avoid it strictly. Soy allergy is more common in young children than adults, and many children outgrow it, but adults with it need the same label discipline as anyone with a food allergy.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Soy and testosterone</h3>

          <p>
            The worry that soy protein lowers testosterone or raises estrogen in men comes mainly from animal studies and isolated case reports involving very large intakes. Meta-analyses of human clinical trials, including a 2021 review in Reproductive Toxicology, have found no meaningful effect of soy protein or isoflavone intake on testosterone or estrogen levels in men at normal dietary amounts. Soy isolate also supports muscle gain alongside resistance training, though most comparisons find whey slightly ahead per gram. If hormones are your only reason to avoid soy, the evidence does not require it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Soy and thyroid medication</h3>

          <p>
            Soy can reduce the absorption of levothyroxine, the standard thyroid hormone replacement, if taken close together. The usual advice is to separate the medication from soy foods by several hours rather than avoid soy altogether, but follow your doctor's or pharmacist's instructions. People with hypothyroidism who are not on medication generally do not need to avoid soy if they get enough iodine. If you take any thyroid medication, check your protein powder plans with your doctor.
          </p>

          <p>
            Other reasons, such as following an elimination diet, avoiding genetically modified crops or simply disliking soy's taste, are personal choices rather than health requirements. All of them are easy to honor with the powders above, and none of the picks costs much more than its soy-containing equivalent.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-on-amazon" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Buying Soy-Free Protein Powder on Amazon: Lecithin, Labels and Listings
          </h2>

          <p>
            Soy-free shopping on Amazon has one specific trap: lecithin source is a detail that listing copy rarely mentions, and it can differ between flavors of the same product. A whey brand can use sunflower lecithin in one flavor and soy in another, or switch suppliers between production runs, and the listing title will not change. Some retail listings even print sunflower and/or soy lecithin, which tells you the brand reserves the right to use either.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Read the label image for every flavor</h3>

          <p>
            Do not assume that because chocolate is soy-free, vanilla is too. Open the listing images for the exact flavor and size you are selecting, zoom in on the ingredient list and the Contains line, and look for the word sunflower before lecithin. If the image is missing or blurry, check the brand's own product page. Then read the actual tub when it arrives, before you open it, because Amazon images lag reformulations.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Buy from the brand or Amazon</h3>

          <p>
            Check the Ships from and Sold by lines under the Add to Cart button. Sold by the brand or by Amazon is safest. Third-party resellers can be legitimate, but they are where counterfeit tubs and old stock with outdated formulas tend to appear, and an old formula can carry a different emulsifier. For an allergy-driven purchase, that risk is not worth a small saving.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lot numbers, expiry and Subscribe & Save</h3>

          <p>
            Note the lot number and best-by date when the tub arrives. If a soy-free product ever triggers a reaction, the lot number is what lets the brand trace the batch and its ingredient suppliers. Subscribe & Save typically saves 5 percent, and up to 15 percent with five or more subscriptions in a month, which is worthwhile on a powder you know you tolerate. But check each delivery's label: a subscription keeps shipping through a reformulation without telling you.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Watch the size selector and work out cost per 25 g</h3>

          <p>
            On many listings the price shown at the top belongs to a different size or flavor from the one in the main image, so confirm the selection before comparing. Then divide the price by servings, divide by protein per serving and multiply by 25 to get cost per 25 g of protein. Klean Isolate's 20 g scoop and OWYN's two-scoop serving both look cheaper per tub than they are per gram. Our <a href="/tools/protein-cost-calculator/">protein cost calculator</a> does the arithmetic, and our <a href="/amazon-protein-powder/">Amazon protein powder</a> guide covers the platform's wider quirks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="allergen-free" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Soy-Free and Free of Other Major Allergens
          </h2>

          <p>
            Many soy-free shoppers are managing more than one allergy. The US now recognizes nine major food allergens: milk, eggs, fish, shellfish, tree nuts, peanuts, wheat, soybeans and, since January 2023 under the FASTER Act, sesame. Protein powders can contain several of them, and flavor systems add more, such as peanut flour in peanut butter flavors or wheat-based cookie pieces in cookies and cream.
          </p>

          <p>
            For soy plus milk, the plant picks and Naked Egg are the options. For soy plus egg, any of the whey picks works if milk is fine. For soy plus milk plus egg, a pea or pea and rice powder is the main route, and OWYN, which states it avoids dairy, soy, gluten, peanuts, tree nuts and sesame, is designed for exactly that household. For gluten as well, our <a href="/best-gluten-free-protein-powder/">best gluten-free protein powder</a> guide covers certified options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Allergen-free claims are only as good as the manufacturing behind them. A powder can have no soy ingredient and still be made on a line that runs soy. If you manage several serious allergies, prefer brands that state their allergen controls clearly, ask about shared equipment, and agree on a specific product with your allergist. For children with multiple food allergies, protein supplementation should go through their pediatrician or a registered dietitian.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more overlap is worth knowing. Soy-free does not mean low in additives. Some soy-free plant powders use gums, sugar alcohols and natural flavors to cover taste, which bothers some sensitive stomachs. If you need soy-free and gentle on the gut, our sibling guide to the <a href="/best-low-fodmap-protein-powder/">best low FODMAP protein powder</a> looks at those additives in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-picked" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. How We Picked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We started with protein powders genuinely sold on Amazon US and kept only those with no soy ingredient and no soy allergen statement on the label or brand listing at the time of writing, including the emulsifier. We then ranked them on protein per serving, protein quality, ingredient simplicity, published certifications such as NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Choice, and cost per 25 g of protein. We did not run lab tests or taste panels; texture notes summarize consistent buyer reports. Our <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test</a> page explains the method.
          </p>

          <p>
            We excluded Garden of Life Sport and Huel Black Edition on the basis of the published heavy-metal results in our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-heavy-metals/">protein powder without heavy metals</a> guide, and we left out products whose listings showed conflicting soy and sunflower lecithin information. If your main goal is a short, clean label rather than soy avoidance specifically, our <a href="/best-clean-protein-powder/">best clean protein powder</a> list overlaps heavily with this one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Checking for soy protein but not soy lecithin</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most soy in protein powder is not soy protein but soy lecithin, the emulsifier that helps whey mix. Several best-selling wheys carry a Contains: Milk and Soy statement for that reason. Read the Contains line and look for sunflower before the word lecithin.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming every flavor of a brand matches</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Lecithin source can differ between flavors and production runs of the same product, and some listings say sunflower and/or soy lecithin. Check the label image for the exact flavor and size you are buying, then read the tub itself before opening it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reading may contain as a complete warning</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Advisory statements about shared facilities are voluntary in the US, so a label without one can still be made on a line that runs soy. With a severe soy allergy, ask the brand directly about shared equipment and agree on a product with your allergist.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Avoiding soy for hormones and overpaying</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Meta-analyses of human trials have found that normal soy intake does not meaningfully change testosterone or estrogen in men. If hormones are your only reason, avoiding soy is optional. If you prefer soy-free anyway, sunflower-lecithin wheys cost about the same.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting dairy when switching to whey</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Swapping a soy shake for a soy-free whey solves the soy problem and creates a dairy one for anyone with a milk allergy or a vegan diet. If both soy and dairy are off the menu, choose a pea-based blend such as Vega Sport or OWYN, or egg white.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

